  "main_findings": [
    "Every declared-universe identified release decision with proper G becomes unresolved under unrestricted same-G BAM decomposition closure.",
    "Declared-universe decision identification therefore depends on restrictions placed on admissible A/B/M state families; it is never an unconditional consequence of current G alone except when G fills the node universe.",
    "The nearest opposite-decision completion is often close: margin 1 for 439/618 proper-G A decisions, 263/671 B decisions and 281/698 M decisions.",
    "No-expansion conclusions were especially fragile in the frozen panel: every finite no-expansion margin was 1 or 2.",
    "Expansion conclusions could have much larger margins because every existing outside-G release witness must be removed to reverse the decision; maxima were 13 for A, 29 for B and 31 for M.",
    "This produces an exact witness asymmetry: creating one new release witness can reverse no-expansion, whereas reversing expansion requires destroying all current release witnesses."
  ],
  "claim_boundary": [
    "Completion margin is logical sensitivity to undeclared same-G decompositions, not a probability or ecological-plausibility score.",
    "Unrestricted decomposition closure is a stress-test envelope, not a recommended ecological candidate universe.",
    "The Phase-II finite-universe decision-identification counts remain exact conditional on their declared universe.",
    "Large margins do not prove the omitted completions are biologically implausible; small margins do not prove they are plausible.",
    "The results do not establish generic novelty for robust decision analysis or structural uncertainty."
  ]
}
